le': False, 'views': {'count': '110', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4Nzg2NDE3MzAzMjY4NzYxNjA=', 'text': 'Nope! Sorry @Jumpman23 Human rights are more important than being a shill for shoes.  I\'m the Michael Jordan of video games and I\'d rather die of starvation than use my clout for $$ when I could be saving lives\n\nNikes lead to Nukes by Nike Sites.\n\nWhat is a Nike Site?\n\nNuclear bases in the United States were colloquially called "Nike Sites" because they were part of the Nike missile defense system during the Cold War. The term "Nike" comes from the Goddess of Victory in Greek mythology, which was the name given to this line of surface-to-air missiles developed by the U.S. Army. These missile systems were deployed to defend against potential Soviet bomber attacks.\nKey Facts about Nike Sites:\n\nPurpose:Nike missile sites were designed to intercept and destroy incoming enemy aircraft, particularly strategic bombers that might carry nuclear weapons.\n\nTypes of Missiles:Nike Ajax: The first missile in the system, introduced in the 1950s.\nNike Hercules: A more advanced version that could carry nuclear warheads and was capable of targeting entire formations of bombers.\n\nDeployment:Hundreds of Nike sites were strategically positioned around major U.S. cities, industrial centers, and military installations. These sites were often located on the outskirts of urban areas.\n\nStructure of a Nike Site:Launch Area: Contained underground missile silos or above-ground launchers.\nControl Area: Housed radar and tracking equipment for guidance.\n\nHistorical Context:Nike sites were a critical part of U.S. defense during the Cold War (1950s–1970s), reflecting the fear of Soviet nuclear aggression.\nAs intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) became the primary threat, Nike systems were gradually decommissioned in favor of other defense technologies.\n\nToday, many former Nike sites have been abandoned, repurposed, or turned into historical landmarks. Some remain as reminders of the Cold War and the military strategies of the time.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '198975780', 'name': 'Jordan', 'screen_name': 'Jumpman23', 'indices': [12, 22]}]}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Mon Jan 13 03:14:26 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1878640629624000777', 'display_text_range': [14, 291], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'media': [{'display_url': 'pic.x.com/PZDJ7rSvkV', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/JamesSager/status/1878641730553278468/photo/1', 'id_str': '1878641722936209408', 'indices': [292, 315], 'media_key': '3_1878641722936209408', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GhJGsYvXMAAgvp-.jpg', 'type': 'photo', 'url': 'https://t.co/PZDJ7rSvkV', 'ext_media_availability': {'status': 'Available'}, 'features': {'large': {'faces': [{'x': 119, 'y': 58, 'h': 84, 'w': 84}]}, 'medium': {'faces': [{'x': 119, 'y': 58, 'h': 84, 'w': 84}]}, 'small': {'faces': [{'x': 119, 'y': 58, 'h': 84, 'w': 84}]}, 'orig': {'faces': [{'x': 119, 'y': 58, 'h': 84, 'w': 84}]}}, 'sizes': {'large': {'h': 199, 'w': 253, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'medium': {'h': 199, 'w': 253, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'small': {'h': 199, 'w': 253, 'resize': 'fit'}, 'thumb': {'h': 150, 'w': 150, 'resize': 'crop'}}, 'original_info': {'height': 199, 'width': 253, 'focus_rects': [{'x': 0, 'y': 57, 'w': 253, 'h': 142}, {'x': 8, 'y': 0, 'w': 199, 'h': 199}, {'x': 20, 'y': 0, 'w': 175, 'h': 199}, {'x': 57, 'y': 0, 'w': 100, 'h': 199}, {'x': 0, 'y': 0, 'w': 253, 'h': 199}]}, 'media_results': {'result': {'media_key': '3_1878641722936209408'}}}], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_